NNAAMMEE
       wall - write a message to all users

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       wwaallll [--nn] [--tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t] [--gg _g_r_o_u_p] [_m_e_s_s_a_g_e | _f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       wwaallll displays a _m_e_s_s_a_g_e, or the contents of a _f_i_l_e, or otherwise its
       standard input, on the terminals of all currently logged in users. The
       command will wrap lines that are longer than 79 characters. Short lines
       are whitespace padded to have 79 characters. The command will always
       put a carriage return and new line at the end of each line.

       Only the superuser can write on the terminals of users who have chosen
       to deny messages or are using a program which automatically denies
       messages.

       Reading from a _f_i_l_e is refused when the invoker is not superuser and
       the program is set-user-ID or set-group-ID.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --nn, ----nnoobbaannnneerr
           Suppress the banner.

       --tt, ----ttiimmeeoouutt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
           Abandon the write attempt to the terminals after _t_i_m_e_o_u_t seconds.
           This _t_i_m_e_o_u_t must be a positive integer. The default value is 300
           seconds, which is a legacy from the time when people ran terminals
           over modem lines.

       --gg, ----ggrroouupp _g_r_o_u_p
           Limit printing message to members of group defined as a _g_r_o_u_p
           argument. The argument can be group name or GID.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

NNOOTTEESS
       Some sessions, such as wwddmm(1x), that have in the beginning of uuttmmpp(5)
       ut_type data a ':' character will not get the message from wwaallll. This
       is done to avoid write errors.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       A wwaallll command appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.

